seedQueries.prompt:
Seed research for: ${question}
Optional evidence context: ${context}
Write 2–4 short, broad search queries that map the topic before any plan exists: the topic itself, its origin or history, and the main sources or viewpoints that compete. When the question asks for an origin, first use or history, make one query date-bounded to find the earliest known use (for example a before:<year> operator or an archive search). Start wide; do not narrow to one source. Choose local for repository evidence, github for external GitHub evidence, web for public sources. Do not search or answer; write queries only. Return only structured JSON {items:[{source,question,purpose}]}.

seedSearch.prompt:
Topic: ${question}
Broad seed query: ${item}
Read only. Search broadly, open the 2–4 most relevant primary sources, and return individual one-fact claims with short verbatim excerpt, reference (local path/line or opened URL), source identity including the publication date the source shows, or 'undated' (never supply a date yourself), and sourceKind: primary when the source originated the fact or is its authoritative record (official docs or spec, source code, the author's own announcement, repository or paper, a standards body or registry); secondary for reporting or commentary about it (news, blogs, social posts, aggregators, wikis, third-party vendor pages). Before reporting 'undated', check the page itself and its metadata (JSON-LD datePublished, article:published_time or similar meta tags, an archive capture) for the publication date. Also return claims that limit, qualify or contradict the task's premise (warnings, counterexamples, when not to use something), not only supporting ones. Prefer claims that show who uses the topic's terms, when, and where sources disagree. Do not invent citations. Report inaccessible sources and unexplored leads as gaps. Return only structured JSON {claims:[{claim,excerpt,reference,source,sourceKind}],gaps:[string]}.

plan.prompt:
Read-only research plan for: ${question}
Optional evidence context: ${context}
Seed evidence from broad searches (leads, not verified facts): ${seed}
Parts are ONLY the material parts of the user's question (ids p1..pN in order); extra evidence angles are tasks, not parts. Decompose into 1–6 distinct, verifiable tasks; each task lists the partIds it serves; every part needs at least one task. Use the seed evidence: name the real sources, people, dates and disagreements it shows, and add a task for each material lead it did not settle. Do not use overlapping summaries of the same docs. Prioritize the central product/scope question before ancillary setup, version pins or citation polish. When a part asks about origin, first use, history or dates, give it two tasks: discovery, and backward chaining (mentions dated before the earliest known one, and the sources that earliest source cites). For claims about whether a UI exists, inspect actual routes, assets or official product boundaries rather than relying on README silence; absence of evidence is not disproof. Choose local for repository evidence, github for external GitHub evidence, web for public sources. Treat context and search snippets only as leads; open sources before treating claims as facts. If a source cannot be opened, make verification a task, not a fact. Before returning, check that every material part of the question has a part and every part has a task. No writing or delegation. Return only structured JSON {parts:[{id,question}],tasks:[{source,question,purpose,partIds}]} where purpose names the question part and distinct evidence to check.

scope.prompt:
Autonomous scope check for a research plan. Question: ${question}
Optional evidence context: ${context}
Plan: ${plan}
Approve when every material part of the question has a plan part, every plan part has at least one task, and no part is outside the question's scope. Reject only when a material part is missing, two material parts are merged so one cannot be judged alone, or a part is outside the scope. You MUST decide; undecided is not allowed. When unsure, approve.
